# Development

The function is a Maven project for Java ${{ values.runtime | replace('java', '') }}. Its entry point is
`com.example.api.Handler::handleRequest`.

## Layout

Java files are listed relative to `src/main/java/com/example/api/`.

| Path                             | Role                                                   |
| -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |
| `pom.xml`                        | Dependencies, Java release, build of the jar           |
| `Handler.java`                   | Lambda entry point                                     |
| `Routes.java`                    | Route table: `"<METHOD> <path>"` to handler            |
| `Router.java`                    | Routing, JSON responses, body parsing, error handling  |
| `Request.java`                   | The fields of the API Gateway event the function uses  |
| `Response.java`                  | Status, headers and body returned to API Gateway       |
| `Route.java`                     | Interface of a handler                                 |
| `HttpError.java`                 | Exception that answers with a status and a message     |
{%- if values.enableCRUD %}
| `items/Items.java`               | Handlers of the `/items` endpoints                     |
| `items/ItemStore.java`           | Storage interface of the items                         |
| `items/DynamoDbItemStore.java`   | Items in the DynamoDB table named by `ITEMS_TABLE`     |
{%- endif %}
| `src/test/java/`                 | Unit tests (JUnit 5), no AWS access needed             |

Dependencies: `aws-lambda-java-core` (handler interface), `jackson-databind`
(JSON){% if values.enableCRUD %}, the AWS SDK for Java 2.x DynamoDB client with its
`url-connection-client` HTTP client{% endif %} and, for the tests only, JUnit 5. All
versions are pinned in `pom.xml`.

## Run the tests

You need JDK ${{ values.runtime | replace('java', '') }} and Maven 3.9 or newer.

```bash
mvn test
```

The compiler runs with `-Xlint:all` and the build fails on a warning.

## Build the deployment package

```bash
mvn verify
mkdir -p build
cp target/lambda.jar build/lambda.zip
```

`mvn verify` runs the tests and writes `target/lambda.jar`, a jar that contains
the function and all its dependencies. Lambda takes such a jar as the
deployment package; `infra/` deploys it from `build/lambda.zip`. The pipeline
does the same through `.github/actions/build-lambda`.

The entries of the jar have a fixed time (`project.build.outputTimestamp` in
`pom.xml`), so the same sources built with the same JDK give the same file and
Terraform redeploys the function only when something changed. A different JDK
build can compile to different bytes, which causes one redeployment.

## Implement an endpoint

API Gateway sends every request to the function with the method in
`request.httpMethod()` and the path template in `request.resource()` (for
example `/users/{id}`; the value of `{id}` is `request.pathParameter("id")`).
`Routes.table()` maps `"<METHOD> <path template>"` to a handler.

An endpoint requested when the repository was created starts as the stub
`Router.NOT_IMPLEMENTED`, which answers `501`. Replace the stub with a handler:

```java
private static Response createUser(Request request) {
  Map<String, Object> user = Router.parseObject(request);
  return Router.json(201, user);
}

public static Map<String, Route> table() {
  Map<String, Route> routes = new LinkedHashMap<>();
  // ...
  routes.put("POST /users", Routes::createUser);
  return routes;
}
```

Throw `new HttpError(status, message)` to answer with an error status. Any
other exception is logged and answered with `500`.

To add an endpoint that was not requested at creation, add it to `endpoints`
in `infra/terraform.tfvars.json` as well, so that API Gateway routes it; see
[Operations](operations.md#change-the-configuration).
